Step by Step Guide for Deployment on onrender.com.

Using GitHub Actions 

To use GitHub Actions to test your code before Render deploys it, you will turn off Render's automatic deployment feature and let GitHub trigger the deployment only after your tests pass.

Here is the step-by-step guide to setting this up.
#
Step 1. Turn Off Render Auto-Deploy

First, prevent Render from deploying automatically every time you push code.

1. Go to your Render Dashboard.
2. Select your Web Service.
3. Click Settings in the left menu.
4. Scroll down to Auto Deploy and change it to No.
5. Click Save Changes.
#
Step 2. Copy Your Render Deploy Hook Render provides a private URL that triggers a deployment when it receives a request.

1. On the same Settings page in Render, scroll to the Deploy Hook sections.
2. Copy the long unique URL provided (it looks like https://render.com...).
#
Step 3. Add the Deploy Hook to GitHub 

Keep your deploy hook URL hidden by saving it as a secret in your GitHub repository.

1. Go to your repository on GitHub. 
2. Click Settings > Secrets and variables > Actions. 
3. Click New repository secret.
4. Name the secret RENDER_DEPLOY_HOOK.
5. Paste your Render Deploy Hook URL into the Secret box.
6. Click Add secret.
#
Step 4. Create the GitHub Actions Workflow 

1. Tell GitHub to run your tests first, and then call Render if the tests succeed.
2. In your project's local code directory, create a folder named .github at the root.
3. Inside it, create another folder named workflows.
4. Create a file inside that folder named ci-cd.yml.
5. Paste the following configuration block into the file:

#
name: Test and Deploy

on:
  push:
    branches: [ main ]

jobs:
  test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Install dependencies and run tests
        run: |
          # Replace these lines with your actual test commands
          npm install
          npm test

  deploy:
    needs: test
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Trigger Render Deploy
        run: |
          curl -X POST "${{ secrets.RENDER_DEPLOY_HOOK }}"

          #
Step 5. Push Your Changes 

1. Commit and push the new workflow file to GitHub:
2. git add .github/workflows/ci-cd.yml
3. git commit -m "Add CI/CD workflow"
4. git push origin main
GitHub will now automatically run your test suite. The deploy step will only execute if the test step completes with zero errors.